  "activity": {
    "id": "retrograde_motion_exploration",
    "name": "Apparent Retrograde Motion of Mars and Outer Planets",
    "description": "An immersive AR lab exploring why Mars and other outer planets appear to reverse direction in the night sky. Students first observe Mars's retrograde path from Earth's perspective, then switch to a heliocentric viewpoint to understand the underlying orbital mechanics. Finally, they compare the retrograde loops of Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn to understand how planetary distance affects apparent retrograde motion.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "retrograde_motion_lab",
    "start": "observe_mars_path",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "observe_mars_path",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "observation_workstation",
        "predicate": "mars_eastward_drift_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Observe Mars's Apparent Motion from Earth",
          "description": "Welcome to the retrograde motion lab! You are standing on Earth, looking up at the night sky. Over the course of weeks, Mars appears to drift slowly eastward against the background of stars\u2014moving from one constellation toward the next. Watch the animation below to see Mars's normal eastward motion. Pay close attention to its steady progression. This is what we observe most of the time, but approximately every 26 months, something puzzling happens."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "mars_retrograde_path",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_eastward_drift_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Mars is moving eastward. Watch this carefully\u2014you'll soon see why it behaves so differently."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "observe_mars_path",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "discover_retrograde_phase",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "observation_workstation",
        "predicate": "mars_retrograde_loop_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Observe Mars's Retrograde Loop",
          "description": "Now watch what happens during the retrograde episode. Mars slows its eastward motion, comes to a stop, and then reverses direction\u2014moving westward across the sky for about two months. This apparent backward motion traces a loop among the stars. After reaching the westernmost point, Mars stops, reverses again, and resumes its normal eastward drift. This westward loop is the central puzzle of planetary motion: why does an outer planet appear to reverse direction?"
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "mars_retrograde_path",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_retrograde_loop_animation",
              "poi": "retrograde_loop_start"
            },
            {
              "target": "observation_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "retrograde_loop_label",
              "text": "Retrograde Loop: ~2 months of westward motion"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "mars_retrograde_path",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_eastward_drift_animation"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "discover_retrograde_phase",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "explain_relative_motion",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "heliocentric_view_zone",
        "predicate": "heliocentric_orbit_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Understand Retrograde Motion: The Heliocentric Explanation",
          "description": "Now let's shift perspective. Imagine you are positioned far above the solar system, looking down at Earth and Mars as they orbit the Sun. Earth orbits closer to the Sun and moves faster on its orbital path. Mars orbits farther out and moves more slowly. Watch the animation: Earth and Mars both move in smooth, counterclockwise orbits. When Earth catches up to and overtakes Mars, something remarkable happens\u2014from Earth's vantage point (on the surface), Mars appears to slow down, stop, and reverse direction. This is exactly like watching a car in the next lane drift backward when your faster car passes it. The retrograde 'loop' is not real; it is an illusion caused by the relative motion of two planets on their separate orbits."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "earth_mars_orbit_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_orbit_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "earth_mars_orbit_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_label",
              "text": "Heliocentric View: Earth overtaking slower Mars creates the illusion"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "mars_retrograde_path",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "The retrograde motion is caused by Earth's faster orbital motion. Watch Earth lap Mars."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "explain_relative_motion",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "compare_models",
        "viewport": "reactions",
        "type": "reactions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "observation_workstation",
        "predicate": "geocentric_vs_heliocentric_video",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Geocentric vs. Heliocentric Models: Why Heliocentrism Wins",
          "description": "For centuries, astronomers using a geocentric (Earth-centered) model had to explain retrograde motion with complex epicycles\u2014small circular loops added to planetary orbits to match observations. These epicycles felt artificial and required constant adjustment. The heliocentric (Sun-centered) model of Copernicus and Kepler explains retrograde motion elegantly and naturally, with no extra circles needed. The heliocentric model is preferred not just because it is correct, but because it is vastly simpler and more elegant\u2014a principle known as parsimony. Watch the comparison video to see how the two models differ."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "observation_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geocentric_vs_heliocentric_video"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "earth_mars_orbit_model",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "compare_models",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "reactions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "compare_outer_planets",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "heliocentric_view_zone",
        "predicate": "jupiter_saturn_loops_video",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Compare Retrograde Loops: Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn",
          "description": "Retrograde motion occurs for all outer planets, but with important differences. Mars, which orbits relatively close to Earth, exhibits a large retrograde loop lasting about 2 months and covering a wide area of the sky. Jupiter is farther out; its retrograde loop is smaller and covers less ground. Saturn is even farther; its loop is smaller still. Why? Because farther planets move more slowly and have a smaller relative velocity difference with respect to Earth. When Earth overtakes a distant planet, the geometry of the overtaking produces a less dramatic apparent backward motion. Watch the comparison of Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn to see this effect, then listen to an explanation of the physics behind it."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "outer_planets_comparison",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "jupiter_saturn_loops_video",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "outer_planets_comparison",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "planet_comparison_audio"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "observation_workstation",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Notice how the loops get smaller and smaller as planets are farther from the Sun."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "compare_outer_planets",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "finish_lab",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "heliocentric_view_zone",
        "predicate": "retrograde_explanation_image",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Summary: The Puzzle Solved",
          "description": "You have now explored retrograde motion from two perspectives: the geocentric view from Earth and the heliocentric view from space. You have seen that apparent retrograde motion is a natural consequence of orbital mechanics\u2014the faster Earth overtakes slower outer planets, and the geometry of their relative positions creates an illusion of backward motion. You have also learned that the heliocentric model explains this phenomenon far more simply and elegantly than the geocentric model. Finally, you have compared how Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn exhibit different sizes of retrograde loops based on their orbital distances. Congratulations! You have solved one of the great puzzles that helped overturn the geocentric model and usher in the modern understanding of the solar system."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_view_zone",
